---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureCompressToSize from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfSecureCompressToSize.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-2mb">
  <PdfSecureCompressToSize client:load targetBytes={2097152} />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>The target for long documents</h2>
      <p>
        At 2 MB, a long document (a thesis chapter, a full set of bank statements, a surveyor's report,
        a scanned lease with schedules) fits an upload limit and stays readable. Below about 40 pages
        the budget is comfortable; above that it gets tight, and the tool tells you when it is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The page-count arithmetic</h2>
      <p>
        The algorithm depends on one division. Do it in your head before you start:
      </p>
      <pre><code>bytes per page ≈ (target − overhead) ÷ pages
overhead      ≈ 1,200 + 260 × pages</code></pre>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Pages</th>
              <th scope="col">Budget per page at 2 MB</th>
              <th scope="col">Verdict</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">10</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">~209 KB</td>
              <td>Excellent: near-original quality at 150–200 DPI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">40</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">~52 KB</td>
              <td>Good. Clean text at about 110 DPI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">100</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">~21 KB</td>
              <td>Acceptable for reading; small print softens</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">300</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">~7 KB</td>
              <td>Heavily degraded: split the document</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The exact figures for your file, with your numbers, show below the result.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Split first, compress second</h2>
      <p>
        When the arithmetic above gives less than about 20 KB a page, do not compress harder. Two
        100-page halves at 2 MB each give each page four times the budget of one 200-page file at 2 MB,
        and most portals that limit a single attachment accept two.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/split-pdf">Split PDF</a>: break a document into parts at the page numbers you choose.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/extract-pdf-pages">Extract PDF pages</a>: take out only the range that was
          requested.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/delete-pages-from-pdf">Delete pages from PDF</a>: remove blank scanner pages,
          separator sheets and duplicate cover pages before you compress.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Removing four blank pages from a 40-page scan helps more than any quality setting: it gives 10
        per cent more budget to each remaining page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How long it takes</h2>
      <p>
        Each page is rendered once and then encoded up to nine times. Rendering takes most of the time,
        and it scales with page area at the chosen DPI, not with the size of the source file.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A modern laptop: about 3 to 5 pages per second at 110 DPI.</li>
        <li>An older laptop or a phone: about 1 page per second.</li>
        <li>
          At 200 DPI: about three times slower, because there are three times as many pixels to draw
          and encode.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The tool reports progress per page, and the Cancel button stops it immediately. The original
        file does not change, because the tool never writes to it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When 2 MB still is not enough</h2>
      <p>
        If the result panel says the target was missed, one of three things is true:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          The page count is past the limit. Each page needs a few hundred bytes of structure in
          addition to its image data. At several hundred pages, that alone comes close to your target.
        </li>
        <li>
          The pages are very large. An A0 poster or an engineering drawing has ten times the area of
          A4, so it needs ten times the pixels at the same DPI.
        </li>
        <li>
          You forced a high resolution. Set the resolution back to automatic and run it again.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
